## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on ordered subsequences with reconstruction, a common interview pattern that scales gracefully from beginner to moderate.

## Key Concepts
- State dp[i][t] represents best sum ending at i as the t-th pick
- Transition uses max over previous indices; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space/time trade-offs; potential O(nk) optimization via running maxima

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDotSubsequence(A,B,k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][1] = A[i]*B[i]; par[i][1] = -1; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best=-Infinity; let arg=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1] > best){ best=dp[j][t-1]; arg=j; }
      }
      if(best>-Infinity){
        dp[i][t] = best + A[i]*B[i];
        par[i][t] = arg;
      }
    }
  }
  let bestEnd=-1, bestVal=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[i][k] > bestVal){ bestVal=dp[i][k]; bestEnd=i; }
  const indices=[]; let i=bestEnd, t=k;
  while(i>=0 && t>=1){ indices.push(i); i=par[i][t]; t--; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {value: bestVal, indices};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space using prefix maxima per t-1?
- How would you extend to handle k up to n with space-saving tricks?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic programming with a non-adjacency constraint and explicit reconstruction of the deletion indices.

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index to enforce non-adjacency
- Exact deletions constraint (k) and reconstruction
- O(nk) time and O(nk) space (can optimize to O(k) with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumAfterDeletions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const keep = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const del = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  keep[0][0] = A[0];
  if (k >= 1) del[0][1] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      keep[i][t] = Math.max(keep[i-1][t], del[i-1][t]);
      if (keep[i][t] > NEG/2) keep[i][t] += A[i];
      if (t > 0) del[i][t] = keep[i-1][t-1];
    }
  }
  const end = Math.max(keep[n-1][k], del[n-1][k]);
  // Backtracking to collect deleted indices would be added here
  return {maxSum: end, deletedIndices: []};
}
```

